# Target C-II -- stress-light bounded cusps in the fixed-star grid

Date: 2026-10-01
Base dependency: 9499600c4c1e0087bc053567b03253c22f188ae7
Status: proof-only; C-II remains open.

## Theorem

In the fixed-star complete O--O rectangle after the local witness-cell extraction, write

\[
|A'|,|B'|\ge(c_*-o(1))D,\qquad
c_*=\frac{\kappa^4}{16\pi^3C_KM_K},
\]

where \(C_K=\lfloor4K\rfloor+2\) and \(M_K=2\lfloor4K\rfloor+3\). Let

\[
H_0=\left\lceil\frac{8\pi}{c_*}\right\rceil+1.
\]

Use one relative-interior normalized KKT stress on the full actual diameter graph:

\[
\beta_h>0,\qquad \sum_{h\in H}\beta_h=1.
\tag{1}
\]

For every fixed \(L>8/c_*\), each shore contains a family of pairwise separated adjacent selected interfaces of size at least

\[
\boxed{
\left(
\frac{c_*/4-2/L}{H_0+3}-o(1)
\right)D
}
\tag{2}
\]

such that, at every retained interface \(i\),

\[
1\le |H_{sw}(i)|\le H_0,
\tag{3}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\mu_i:=\sum_{h\in H_{sw}(i)}\beta_h\le\frac{L}{D},
}
\tag{4}
\]

and distinct retained interfaces have disjoint adjacent train pairs and disjoint
switching-diameter edge sets.

If the two boundary trains have coherently rooted unit directions \(u_i,u_{i+1}\)
and positive total KKT contact weights \(W_i,W_{i+1}\), their exact middle-layer
reaction is

\[
G_i=W_i u_i-W_{i+1}u_{i+1}.
\tag{5}
\]

For every retained interface,

\[
\boxed{0<|G_i|\le L,}
\tag{6}
\]

the reaction is generated by one or two actual switching bars,

\[
\boxed{
G_i=\lambda_1n_{h_1}+\lambda_2n_{h_2},
\qquad \lambda_j>0,
}
\tag{7}
\]

with the second term omitted in the one-bar case, and

\[
\boxed{|W_i-W_{i+1}|\le L.}
\tag{8}
\]

The selected one/two-bar reaction supports are edge-disjoint across the retained
interfaces.

Taking \(L=16/c_*\) yields on each shore at least

\[
\boxed{
\left(
\frac{c_*}{8(H_0+3)}-o(1)
\right)D
}
\tag{9}
\]

pairwise separated bounded cusps whose entire switching diameter stress is
\(O(D^{-1})\).

## Proof

The bounded-cusp theorem at the base dependency gives at least

\[
\left(\frac{c_*}{4}-o(1)\right)D
\tag{10}
\]

active adjacent interfaces on each shore with at most \(H_0\) switching rows.

Order the selected \(A'\)-cuts along their complement-dual path. Exact laminar
interval support says that the signed incidence sequence of one actual diameter row is
zero, then one constant nonzero sign on a contiguous interval, then zero. Therefore
one diameter row can switch at at most two adjacent selected interfaces.

For interface \(i\), define \(\mu_i\) by (4). Double counting switching incidences
and using the normalized stress (1) gives the exact bound

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_i\mu_i
&=
\sum_h\beta_h\#\{i:h\in H_{sw}(i)\}\\
&\le2\sum_h\beta_h=2.
\end{aligned}
\tag{11}
\]

Hence the number of interfaces with \(\mu_i>L/D\) is at most

\[
\frac{2D}{L}.
\tag{12}
\]

Deleting those from the bounded active family (10) leaves at least

\[
\left(\frac{c_*}{4}-\frac2L-o(1)\right)D
\tag{13}
\]

bounded stress-light interfaces.

Now use the conflict graph from the bounded-cusp theorem. An interface conflicts with
at most two others through a shared boundary train. It contains at most \(H_0\)
switching diameter edges, and each such edge can switch at at most one other selected
interface. Thus the conflict degree is at most \(H_0+2\). A greedy independent set
retains at least a \(1/(H_0+3)\) fraction. This proves (2)--(4), including the
train-pair and switching-edge disjointness. The \(B'\)-shore proof is identical.

Fix one retained interface and let \(R_i\) be its middle source layer. The exact
adjacent-strip force identity is

\[
G_i
=
W_i u_i-W_{i+1}u_{i+1}
=
\sum_{h\in H_{sw}(i)}\beta_h n_h,
\qquad |n_h|=D,
\tag{14}
\]

where \(n_h\) points from the exterior endpoint of the switching diameter into the
middle layer. The audited adjacent-strip reaction-nondegeneracy theorem gives
\(G_i\ne0\). Equation (4) then gives

\[
0<|G_i|
\le
D\sum_{h\in H_{sw}(i)}\beta_h
=D\mu_i
\le L,
\]

proving (6). Since \(G_i\) is a nonzero vector in the positive cone generated by the
switching vectors, the audited planar conic-Caratheodory sparse-reaction theorem gives
the one/two-bar representation (7). Packet edge-disjointness makes these sparse
supports edge-disjoint across retained interfaces.

Finally,

\[
\begin{aligned}
|G_i|^2
&=
|W_i u_i-W_{i+1}u_{i+1}|^2\\
&=
(W_i-W_{i+1})^2
+2W_iW_{i+1}(1-u_i\cdot u_{i+1})\\
&\ge (W_i-W_{i+1})^2,
\end{aligned}
\]

so (6) implies (8).

For \(L=16/c_*\), the coefficient in (13) is \(c_*/8\), and (9) follows.

## Frontier effect

The fixed-star branch now contains linearly many pairwise separated packets on each
shore with all of the following simultaneously:

- two adjacent O--O cuts and the same fixed full-span witness pair;
- between one and \(H_0\) ordered switching diameter bars;
- total normalized switching \(\beta\)-mass \(O(D^{-1})\);
- a nonzero \(O(1)\) exact layer reaction supported by one or two switching bars;
- an \(O(1)\) jump between the adjacent train-force magnitudes;
- the full linear transverse O--O shore of the complete inverse-phase grid.

Thus KKT stress concentration cannot be used to evade the bounded-cusp reduction.
The remaining fixed-star target is a local geometric incompatibility for the one-bar
and two-bar reaction patterns inside the one-arc prefix/hub/suffix order and the common
transverse grid. The split-star and inactive-interface branches remain open.

No computation, verifier, numerical experiment, or CI output is used.
